> so I'm not sure what you mean by worse --- is this versus
> simulated data?
>
> Actually, the predicted y_tilde matches quite well with the y.
>
> But, my issue is more on rho - particularly seems the sd is quite unstable - at iter=2000, rho[3] has a mean and sd of 5.77 and 45.99 respectively. When i increase the iter to 100000, rho1, rho2, rho3 have (mena, sd) of (5.34, 140.49), (6.17, 140.89), and (6.65, 112.82) respectively.
>
> Is this something I can fix through better model/prior specification or is it something I have to live with given the (highly) correlated predictors?

You need to run until the estimates for the posterior means of rho1, rho2, and rho3
have converged.  What's the r-hat and n_eff at 2000 (post-warmup!) iterations?

There are some densities, like the Cauchy, that don't have
finite mean and variance.  It's unlikely to come up in a posterior
unless you have very fat-tailed priors and very little data.

- Bob

