This should usually be reparameterized as

parameters {
  vector[nYears] z;
  real<lower=0> sigmaEta; // don't know why this has Eta in its symbol name
  ...
}
model {
  vector[nYears] epsilon;
  vector[nYears] mu;
  epsilon[1] <- sigmaEta * z[1];

  for (year in 2:nYears) {

    epsilon[year] <- rho * epsilon[year - 1] + sigmaEta * z[year];
  }
  mu <- alpha + tau * yearLessOne + epsilon;
  z ~ normal(0,1); // implies epsilon ~ normal(rho * epsilon[year - 1], sigmaEta)
  ...

> In this model, rho is not recoverable. Usually all or almost all of the epsilon parameter include 0 in their 80% CrI (see example output). I suspect this why Stan does not recover rho. Does this seem like a reasonable explanation?

You mean rho is not identifiable from the data alone? I don't immediately see why that would be. But I could see why there would be too much dependence between epsilon and sigmaEta to obtain good sampling the way you had it originally.

