Have included the text files of the model code and sample data here.

I ran into the following issue when trying to compile the model:

By declaring delta_Dmis[D_mis], delta_Vmis[V_mis], delta_Wmis[W_mis] and delta_Zmis[Z_mis] in the parameter block, they are required to be declared as real type(?). However, in the model block, they come from a binomial/poisson distribution, requiring them to be declared as an integer type.

Any thoughts on how to best resolve this, please?

Many thanks!

Regards,
Cindy

On Thursday, May 28, 2015 at 10:15:05 AM UTC+8, Cindy Lim wrote:
> Dear all,
>
> Thanks so much for your comments! Have realised where I went wrong in the code. Declared I wrongly in the parameter block when it should be in the data block, and have also declared it as an integer type. Have also converted the priors to stan code accordingly.
>
> For missing data, the reference manual suggested coding the observed data and missing data separately, where N_obs is the number of observed data and N_mis is the number of missing data. In this model, the number of missing values differ for D,V,Z and W and are dependent on time. Not sure if I understand the section clearly, but to include missing values, I should be amending the model code chunk to something like the following?
>
> model {
>
> for (j in 1:nobs)
> {
> I[j] ~ poisson(pop*lambda[j]);
>
> delta_D[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pD);
>
> delta_V[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pV);
>
> delta_Z[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pZ);
>
> delta_W[j] ~ poisson(thetaW*pW*I[j]);
> }
>
> for (k in 1:D_mis)
>   {
> delta_Dmis[k] ~ binomial(I[j],pD);
> }
>
> for (k in 1:V_mis)
>   {
> delta_Vmis[k] ~ binomial(I[j],pV);
> }
>
> for (k in 1:Z_mis)
>   {
> delta_Zmis[k] ~ binomial(I[j],pZ);
> }
>
> for (k in 1:W_mis)
>   {
> delta_Wmis[k] ~ poisson(thetaW*pW*I[j]);
> }
>
> }
>
> where D_mis, V_mis, W_mis and Z_mis are the number of missing values in delta_D, delta_V, delta_W and delta_Z, respectively. I will then replace the NAs in the dataset with the parameters delta_Dmis, delta_Vmis, delta_Wmis and delta_Zmis for delta_D, delta_V, delta_W and delta_Z.
>
> Is my understanding correct, please?
>
> Many thanks!
>
> Regards,
> Cindy
>
> On Thursday, May 28, 2015 at 12:59:20 AM UTC+8, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > It
> > > On May 26, 2015, at 11:25 PM, Cindy Lim <cindy...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > >
> > >
> > > Hi,
> > >
> > > I read from the reference manual and previous posts in this group that the Poisson distribution does not support the declaration of a real-valued parameter.
> >
> > Real-valued outcome.  So in
> >
> >   y ~ poisson(lambda);
> >
> > the parameter lambda is real, but the outcome y is a count value (integer >= 0).
> >
> > > I converted a logistic growth JAGS model to STAN code as below. Lambda is calculated based on the parameters K,Q and b, which will cause it to be a real rather than an integer. This is then passed on to the Poisson distribution in the model block. I was wondering if there is any way to get around the integer declaration, please?
> >
> > So you can't do this:
> >
> >   real <lower=0> I[nobs];
> >
> >   I[j] ~ poisson(pop*lambda[j]);
> >
> > because the outcome must be an integer.
> >
> > That's just how the Poisson works.  You could replace the Poisson with
> > a positive-constrained real-valued density, but that's not going to plug
> > into the binomial.
> >
> > I'd suggest looking into the negative binomial if you want to model
> > overdispersion.
> >
> > - Bob
> >
> >
> > >
> > > Any advice would be much appreciated!
> > >
> > > Regards,
> > > Cindy
> > >
> > >
> > > #######################
> > > ###Model code for RSTAN
> > > #######################
> > >
> > > data {
> > > int<lower=0> nobs;
> > > int<lower=0> delta_D[nobs];
> > > int<lower=0> delta_V[nobs];
> > > int<lower=0> delta_W[nobs];
> > > int<lower=0> delta_Z[nobs];
> > > int<lower=0> T1[nobs];
> > > int<lower=0> T2[nobs];
> > > int<lower=0> pop;
> > >
> > > }
> > >
> > >
> > > parameters {
> > > real alphaD;
> > > real alphaV;
> > > real alphaW;
> > > real alphaZ;
> > > real betaD;
> > > real betaV;
> > > real betaW;
> > > real betaZ;
> > >
> > > real muK;
> > > real muQ;
> > > real mub;
> > >
> > > real<lower=0> sigmaK;
> > > real<lower=0> sigmaQ;
> > > real<lower=0> sigmab;
> > >
> > > real thetaW;
> > > real thetaK;
> > > real thetaQ;
> > > real thetab;
> > >
> > > real <lower=0> I[nobs];
> > >
> > > real<lower=0,upper=1> pD;
> > > real<lower=0,upper=1> pV;
> > > real<lower=0,upper=1> pW;
> > > real<lower=0,upper=1> pZ;
> > >
> > > }
> > >
> > >
> > > transformed parameters {
> > >
> > > real K;
> > > real Q;
> > > real b;
> > > real lambda[nobs];
> > > K<-exp(thetaK);
> > > Q<-exp(thetaQ);
> > > b<-exp(thetab);
> > >
> > > for (j in 1:nobs)
> > > {
> > > lambda[j] <- 0.001 + (K/(Q*exp(-b*T2[j])+1))-(K/(Q*exp(-b*T1[j])+1));
> > > }
> > >
> > > }
> > >
> > >
> > > model {
> > >
> > > for (j in 1:nobs)
> > > {
> > > I[j] ~ poisson(pop*lambda[j]);
> > >
> > > delta_D[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pD);
> > >
> > > delta_V[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pV);
> > >
> > > delta_Z[j] ~ binomial(I[j],pZ);
> > >
> > > delta_W[j] ~ poisson(thetaW*pW*I[j]);
> > > }
> > >
> > >
> > > thetaK ~ dnorm(muK,sigmaK);
> > > thetaQ ~ dnorm(muQ,sigmaQ);
> > > thetab ~ dnorm(mub,sigmab);
> > >
> > > pD ~ dbeta(alphaD,betaD);
> > > pV ~ dbeta(alphaV,betaV);
> > > pZ ~ dbeta(alphaZ,betaZ);
> > > pW ~ dbeta(alphaW,betaW);
> > >
